WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.880
If you live to be 80, you'll have about

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:10.160
22,000 evenings as an adult. That is

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:11.880
your entire allotment. The question is,

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:14.120
what do you [music] do with them? Your

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:16.680
evenings decide what kind of life you

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:18.880
wake up to. But most ambitious people

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:21.560
are wasting time. For those of you who

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:24.000
are new here, I have been a CEO,

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:26.120
investor, board member in tech companies

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.600
that generated billions in value. And I

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:31.440
want to show you a system that helps you

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:33.960
stop wasting your evenings and weekends

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:36.800
[music] and make time for everything. By

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:39.320
the end, you'll know how to achieve more

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:41.360
out of one well-run week than most

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:43.640
people get out of a year. The most

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:45.960
important number in your bank account is

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:48.720
not on your bank statement. It's the

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:51.200
number 1,437.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:54.040
That's the number of free or leisure

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:57.080
hours a full-time worker in America gets

00:00:54.040 --> 00:01:01.480
every year. That's according to BLS. You

00:00:57.080 --> 00:01:03.640
get about 3.18 hours per weekday and 5.8

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:05.680
hours per weekend or [music] holiday.

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:08.160
And this is what's surprising. The

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:10.880
average worker puts in about 45

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:14.400
workweeks a year. And if you add up all

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:17.080
your free time, it's almost 36 workweeks

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:19.800
every year. So, your after-work hours

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:22.880
are not some tiny leftover. They're

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:25.240
almost another career hiding inside your

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:28.040
year. But those hidden hours do not

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:30.920
automatically become a better life,

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:34.480
right? They arrive with hope and they

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:36.720
often end with a quiet shame of knowing

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:39.080
that you had time, but you did not

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:42.120
really have control over it. That is why

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:44.200
attention matters. You own your time

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:47.160
only when you own your attention. During

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:50.160
the day, your role gives you guardrails.

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:51.640
From 9:00 to 5:00, you have a calendar.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:54.240
You have a team. You have a job. You

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:56.640
have a role. And all of that constantly

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:59.040
tell you how to manage your attention

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:01.720
and your time. Then comes the evening

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:03.040
and those guardrails disappear. Now,

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:06.280
you're free,

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:08.440
but is your attention actually free? The

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:12.000
phrase free time makes us think these

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:14.120
hours cost nothing, but every hour has

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.120
an opportunity cost. So, the best way to

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:19.000
think about your evening is like a bank

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:21.640
account. Some hours are deposits and

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:23.640
some hours are withdrawals. Sleep,

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:26.680
creative work, time spent with your

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:29.320
loved ones, all a great deposit. What if

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:31.640
you have doom scrolling, social media,

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:34.720
withdrawal? But, what if you have had a

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:36.680
rough day and you need to unwind? If all

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:39.360
you want to do is binge watch Grey's

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:41.560
Anatomy or Bridgerton, are you

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:44.560
withdrawing from your bank account? And

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:47.560
sure, sometimes you have to unwind and

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:49.640
be a total zombie. But, here's the trap.

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:52.040
There is a research from the Journal of

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:54.320
Applied Psychology paper called Did You

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:57.160
Have a Nice Evening? They found that

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:59.440
relaxing and recovery are not the same.

00:02:57.160 --> 00:03:01.640
If you had a stressful day, it's better

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:04.600
to choose a form of recovery that

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:07.640
changes your state. Cook something or

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:10.720
practice music. Read 10 pages of a book.

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:13.480
Go for a walk. That is a deposit. And

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:16.120
the debt in the bank account is a little

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:18.880
more subtle. When I work late at night,

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:21.120
I feel like I have to squeeze everything

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:23.519
I can out of the last few hours of the

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:25.920
day. The house is quiet, nobody is

00:03:23.519 --> 00:03:28.920
interrupting me. I have focus, I have

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:31.600
momentum. I stay up late, really late.

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:33.959
And the next morning, I pay for it. That

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:35.320
is my ambition turning into a debt. The

00:03:33.959 --> 00:03:37.800
first action item is pretty

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:40.000
straightforward. Audit your evening bank

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:42.040
account. Make a list of your deposits,

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:43.600
your withdrawals, your debts. Because

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:46.360
just like a bank account, if your

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:48.680
deposits exceed your withdrawals over

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:51.400
time, you keep growing and compounding.

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:53.480
The real question is if your evening is

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:56.520
a hidden bank account, what is the best

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:59.320
way to invest in it? How do you decide

00:03:56.520 --> 00:04:01.280
whether tonight should be about work,

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:04.400
about rest, about family, about

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:07.400
learning, or just about shutting down?

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:11.640
You need a decisioning framework. I call

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:14.280
it the 3C framework. Clock, compass, and

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:17.000
climate. Clock is about what kind of

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:19.320
work fits your biology. Compass, which

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:22.240
accounts need funding? Your physical

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:24.680
body, or your emotional bonds, or your

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:27.840
big career bets. And the climate, what

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:29.560
zone you're in. All of those things will

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:31.440
change the way you think about your

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:33.560
evening. Let's start with the first C,

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:35.840
clock. Are you a morning person or a

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:37.640
night owl? Waking up at 5:00 a.m. alone

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:39.720
does not make you a great leader. Tim

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:41.560
Cook used to wake up at 4:00 a.m. every

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:44.160
morning. And Barack Obama and Winston

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:46.680
Churchill were famously night owls who

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:50.640
stayed up way past midnight. Your best

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:53.080
hour is not the hour some billionaire or

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:56.600
a YouTuber tells you to use. It depends

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:59.640
on what your chronotype is. A chronotype

00:04:56.600 --> 00:05:02.360
is basically your biological rhythm.

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:04.440
Some people naturally come alive early

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:06.760
in the morning. Some people, like me,

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:08.840
come alive later. Some people are

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:11.320
somewhere in the middle. The point is to

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:14.440
know when your brain actually works

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:16.840
best. If your best hour is 1:00 a.m.,

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:19.640
use it. But only if your life lets you

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:22.560
protect the sleep on the other side.

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:25.560
Chronotype is not a permission to steal

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:28.000
from tomorrow. It's a permission to stop

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:30.520
following someone else's clock. I know

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:33.600
of plenty of night owls who work late

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:36.440
and sleep late. That's why advice like

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:39.560
just go to bed early or wake up early is

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:41.840
useless to many of us. The lesson is

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:45.680
stop borrowing from someone else's

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:47.800
biology. Match the work to your clock.

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:50.200
Ask yourself, what are the hours when

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:52.440
you're the sharpest? Use that for

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:55.360
precision work. What are the hours when

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:58.360
your brain is more uninhibited and open

00:05:55.360 --> 00:06:00.600
to associative thinking? Use those hours

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:02.600
for imagination. What are the hours

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:03.560
where you are completely tired and

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:06.560
depleted? [music]

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:08.800
Use them for closure and recovery. In

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:11.520
terms of action items, you don't have to

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:13.440
do much except reflect. You probably

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:15.920
already know more than you think. You

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:18.760
know what your chronotype is. When do

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:21.800
you usually make clear decisions, clean

00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:25.240
decisions? And when do you get your best

00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:26.560
ideas? And when do you start making dumb

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:28.880
mistakes?

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:31.480
That's your clock. And as long as you're

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:34.280
not asking your tired brain to do your

00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:35.240
sharpest thinking, you'll be fine. And

00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:38.400
remember, [music]

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:41.080
at night when you get that second wind,

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:43.720
don't mistake treating it like a second

00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:46.680
day. And now, get to the second C in the

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:49.680
framework, your compass. When I was

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:52.160
young, my first job was in Boston. And

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:54.680
it was in a very small, dark, dingy

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:56.840
little room full of cables and servers

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:59.480
and hardware and computer parts and

00:06:56.840 --> 00:07:01.640
three other guys who were as unhappy as

00:06:59.480 --> 00:07:05.080
they were stuck. I was working as a help

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:07.560
desk person. But for me, that room was

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:10.720
such a gift because I didn't come from

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:12.800
tech. So on evenings and weekends, I

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:15.520
studied everything I could about

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:17.400
technology, the operating systems,

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:20.080
networking protocols, programming

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:22.280
languages, anything. And I read

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:24.680
everything about communications and

00:07:22.280 --> 00:07:27.160
collaborations and soft skills and hard

00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:29.360
skills. I would reach out to senior

00:07:27.160 --> 00:07:31.080
leaders of the company and say, "Hey, do

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:33.320
you have any extra projects that I can

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:36.840
take?" And I would do it after hours.

00:07:33.320 --> 00:07:38.840
One of those leaders was the EVP of

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:41.520
advanced technologies. His name was Dr.

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:44.240
Paul Abramson. And he had two PhDs from

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:45.710
MIT. For some reason,

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:46.120
he took a liking to me,

00:07:45.710 --> 00:07:48.680
>> [music]

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:50.640
>> and he became one of my earliest mentors

00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:52.600
in corporate America.

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:54.560
When I applied to MIT, he wrote a

00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:56.160
wonderful recommendation letter for me.

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:57.640
And he even guided me through the

00:07:56.160 --> 00:08:00.320
application process. [music]

00:07:57.640 --> 00:08:03.320
Now, looking back, I can see it now that

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:06.280
my future was built very quietly, one

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:08.360
evening and one weekend at a time. But,

00:08:06.280 --> 00:08:10.880
let me be honest about this, too,

00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:14.240
that it worked because at that point of

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:16.200
my life, I was single. I had very few

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:18.919
obligations outside of work. Two of my

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:21.200
best friends were buried in medical

00:08:18.919 --> 00:08:24.200
school and neuroscience. My evenings and

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:27.120
my weekends were usually open. So, I

00:08:24.200 --> 00:08:29.640
just doubled down on investing in skills

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:32.800
and a better future for myself, building

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:35.400
networks. Now, that is what I mean by

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:37.400
compass. Your evening compass has to

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:39.360
have a clear direction. Typically, there

00:08:37.400 --> 00:08:41.960
are three directions that I think of.

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:44.920
Physical direction is anything that

00:08:41.960 --> 00:08:47.400
gives you better health, energy,

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:50.360
capacity. Emotional directions are

00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:51.839
actions that bring you closer to people

00:08:50.360 --> 00:08:54.640
who matter to you.

00:08:51.839 --> 00:08:57.240
And vocational direction, where you make

00:08:54.640 --> 00:09:00.120
future-facing investments, skills,

00:08:57.240 --> 00:09:02.240
projects, relationships. Now, they may

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:04.520
not pay off tomorrow morning, but can

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:06.880
change the direction of your future life

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:10.160
tremendously. But, here's a trap that

00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:12.720
most ambitious people miss. I did. I was

00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:15.280
living in a suburb north of Boston, and

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:17.839
the winters were super harsh, and the

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:20.880
loneliness was harsher. And it would get

00:09:17.839 --> 00:09:23.720
dark at 4:00 p.m. in the afternoon. Now,

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:26.240
fortunately, I had trained to be a monk.

00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:28.680
I had good enough tolerance for

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:31.166
solitude, but I should have invested

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:34.200
more in my emotional relationships

00:09:31.166 --> 00:09:36.440
[music] and in exercise. If your body

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:38.880
has been ignored, your success will

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:39.880
become too heavy a burden for you to

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:42.120
carry.

00:09:39.880 --> 00:09:45.040
If your relationships are ignored, your

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:47.480
success will get very lonely. Early in

00:09:45.040 --> 00:09:50.240
your career, many mornings will start in

00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:52.600
the same dark rooms like mine did. But

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:55.080
what changes your life is what you do in

00:09:52.600 --> 00:09:56.400
the evenings. And now, the final C, the

00:09:55.080 --> 00:09:58.880
climate.

00:09:56.400 --> 00:10:02.080
Sometimes your normal evening routine

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:04.320
will not survive contact with reality.

00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:06.760
Peace time evenings and war time

00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:08.840
evenings are not the same thing. So your

00:10:06.760 --> 00:10:09.960
evenings should change when your climate

00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:12.280
changes.

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:15.840
When Google's Gemini started gaining

00:10:12.280 --> 00:10:18.680
momentum, the CEO of OpenAI, Sam Altman,

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:21.000
reportedly declared a code red inside

00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:24.240
the company. Till then, OpenAI had built

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:27.560
a massive lead in terms of 800 million

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:30.200
users worldwide. They had become a verb

00:10:27.560 --> 00:10:32.680
that replaced AI. Just like people used

00:10:30.200 --> 00:10:35.480
to say, "Google it." People started

00:10:32.680 --> 00:10:37.600
saying, "Oh, I'll just ChatGPT it." When

00:10:35.480 --> 00:10:40.280
the sleeping giant awoke, Google

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:42.240
delivered Gemini 3.0 that was miles

00:10:40.280 --> 00:10:44.800
ahead of anything else at that point,

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:47.120
and it was war time. OpenAI had to

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:49.600
refocus all of their energy on their

00:10:47.120 --> 00:10:52.080
core engine. They delayed or canceled a

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:55.520
lot of their initiatives like ads,

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:58.200
agents, [music] pulse, Sora, so that all

00:10:55.520 --> 00:11:00.480
the energy could be redirected to the

00:10:58.200 --> 00:11:02.839
single battle front,

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:05.360
ChatGPT. That's what war time looks

00:11:02.839 --> 00:11:08.839
like. And I can't imagine that Sam

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:10.640
Altman had time to play piano during his

00:11:08.839 --> 00:11:12.760
war time evenings.

00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:15.480
When the climate changes, the old

00:11:12.760 --> 00:11:18.560
routine becomes the wrong routine. You

00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:21.120
choose what matters most, and everything

00:11:18.560 --> 00:11:23.080
else can wait. A founder raising money

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:25.200
for his startup, for example, does not

00:11:23.080 --> 00:11:27.720
have the same evening as someone in a

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:29.640
stable career. A mother with a newborn

00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:32.160
does not have the same evening as a

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:34.360
mother with a 12-year-old. A hedge fund

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:36.320
analyst during the peak earning season

00:11:34.360 --> 00:11:38.880
is not going to have the same night as

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:42.280
he did in a quiet month. In peace time,

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:44.640
your evenings can compound. In wartime,

00:11:42.280 --> 00:11:47.560
your evenings have only one job,

00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:50.360
survival. Before you plan your evening,

00:11:47.560 --> 00:11:53.120
name your climate. Ask, "Am I in peace

00:11:50.360 --> 00:11:56.160
time right now or in wartime climate?"

00:11:53.120 --> 00:11:59.160
If it is a peace time, invest away. If

00:11:56.160 --> 00:12:01.400
it's a wartime, narrow the night, narrow

00:11:59.160 --> 00:12:04.320
the fight. But, what if your evening is

00:12:01.400 --> 00:12:06.960
totally crazy and you have no time to do

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:09.520
anything? There is a version of yourself

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:12.640
who finishes work on time, eats a clean

00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:14.800
dinner, trains for 45 minutes, reads 20

00:12:12.640 --> 00:12:16.900
pages of a wonderful book, journals,

00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:19.240
meditates, calls a friend, and sleeps

00:12:16.900 --> 00:12:22.520
[music] about 10:00. But, that version

00:12:19.240 --> 00:12:25.920
does not exist. Real evenings have late

00:12:22.520 --> 00:12:29.040
calls and delayed trains and sick kids

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:31.800
and client fires and mad bosses, dishes,

00:12:29.040 --> 00:12:33.600
and laundry. If your evening only works

00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:36.080
when life is perfect, you don't have a

00:12:33.600 --> 00:12:38.360
system, you have a fiction. But, in

00:12:36.080 --> 00:12:40.320
those wartime zones, when everything is

00:12:38.360 --> 00:12:43.080
going against you, it's better to stop

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:46.320
chasing the perfect evening.

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:48.839
Aim for what I call the enough evening.

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:51.680
Three things for the evening that has

00:12:48.839 --> 00:12:54.600
already gone out of hand. First, remove.

00:12:51.680 --> 00:12:57.040
Barack Obama wore mostly gray or blue

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:59.640
suits as president. Steve Jobs had that

00:12:57.040 --> 00:13:01.760
famous black turtleneck. Anna Wintour

00:12:59.640 --> 00:13:03.920
has kept the same bob and the same

00:13:01.760 --> 00:13:07.440
sunglasses for 30 years. Different

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:10.280
worlds, same move. They have too many

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:12.480
real decisions to make, so they remove

00:13:10.280 --> 00:13:15.200
the small ones. Apply that to your

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:17.680
evening. Ask yourself, "What small

00:13:15.200 --> 00:13:20.760
decisions or distractions can you

00:13:17.680 --> 00:13:23.120
remove?" Second, reflect. Pick three

00:13:20.760 --> 00:13:25.560
questions and ask them every night

00:13:23.120 --> 00:13:27.800
before bed. It'll take you 2 minutes.

00:13:25.560 --> 00:13:29.720
Nowadays, my three favorite questions

00:13:27.800 --> 00:13:32.240
are super simple. What did I learn

00:13:29.720 --> 00:13:34.280
today? What did I improve today in my

00:13:32.240 --> 00:13:36.320
life or someone else's life? And what

00:13:34.280 --> 00:13:39.640
did I smile about today? Because the

00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:43.400
most grounded people I know share one

00:13:39.640 --> 00:13:46.680
habit. Morning for meditation, evening

00:13:43.400 --> 00:13:48.920
for reflection. And third, rest. Neil

00:13:46.680 --> 00:13:52.000
deGrasse Tyson has this great line that

00:13:48.920 --> 00:13:55.720
if aliens came to our planet and watched

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:58.720
us humans lie down semi-comatose

00:13:55.720 --> 00:14:00.600
for 1/3 of Earth's rotation, they would

00:13:58.720 --> 00:14:03.680
wonder what the heck is wrong with these

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:07.320
people. And he's right. Sleep looks

00:14:03.680 --> 00:14:09.440
totally absurd. For a third of the day,

00:14:07.320 --> 00:14:11.800
you lose consciousness, become so

00:14:09.440 --> 00:14:16.480
vulnerable, and produce absolutely

00:14:11.800 --> 00:14:19.240
nothing. And yet, evolution chose sleep.

00:14:16.480 --> 00:14:21.880
That should humble every productivity

00:14:19.240 --> 00:14:24.720
addict on Earth. So, protect your sleep.

00:14:21.880 --> 00:14:26.880
First, have a 15-minute landing strip.

00:14:24.720 --> 00:14:29.400
That's your ritual before you go to

00:14:26.880 --> 00:14:31.800
sleep. Read a book, shower, change

00:14:29.400 --> 00:14:34.160
clothes, journal, whatever comes to

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:36.320
mind. And second, protect your sleep

00:14:34.160 --> 00:14:38.480
from your devices. Harvard Medical

00:14:36.320 --> 00:14:41.280
School study found that using our

00:14:38.480 --> 00:14:44.440
devices before bed delays your body

00:14:41.280 --> 00:14:46.800
clock. It suppresses melatonin, cuts REM

00:14:44.440 --> 00:14:49.720
sleep, and leaves you less alert the

00:14:46.800 --> 00:14:51.960
next morning even if you had 8 hours of

00:14:49.720 --> 00:14:55.040
sleep. A part of my enough evening

00:14:51.960 --> 00:14:57.760
ritual is to write a short note to the

00:14:55.040 --> 00:15:00.120
community of folks who think about these

00:14:57.760 --> 00:15:02.520
kind of things. That newsletter, by the

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:05.280
way, goes out every week. Link is in the

00:15:02.520 --> 00:15:07.600
description if you feel like it. 60

00:15:05.280 --> 00:15:11.400
years of adult life,

00:15:07.600 --> 00:15:13.760
roughly 22,000 evenings total.

00:15:11.400 --> 00:15:17.160
That's what you and I have.

00:15:13.760 --> 00:15:19.400
The question is, will they be enough?

00:15:17.160 --> 00:15:23.040
You know, we're called human beings.

00:15:19.400 --> 00:15:25.600
We're terrible at just being.

00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:26.840
The best evenings do not just end the

00:15:25.600 --> 00:15:28.320
day.

00:15:26.840 --> 00:15:29.920
They are enough

00:15:28.320 --> 00:15:31.440
to return you

00:15:29.920 --> 00:15:32.560
to yourself.

00:15:31.440 --> 00:15:35.240
Thank you.

00:15:32.560 --> 00:15:35.240
And I love you.

